{
  "id": "rotate-image",
  "questionId": "rotate-image",
  "slug": "rotate-image",
  "title": "Rotate Image",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Two pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "matrix manipulation",
    "in-place transformation"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "a 90 degree clockwise rotation can be broken into two simpler, well-known operations - what happens if you transpose the matrix first (swap rows and columns)?",
    "after transposing, what single operation on each row turns it into a clockwise rotation?",
    "transpose swaps matrix[r][c] with matrix[c][r]; then reversing every row completes the clockwise rotation",
    "alternatively, rotate layer by layer: for each ring, cycle groups of four cells (top, right, bottom, left) directly into their rotated positions"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "transposing but forgetting to reverse each row afterward (or reversing columns instead of rows)",
    "allocating a new matrix when the problem specifically wants an in-place rotation",
    "off-by-one errors when swapping the four corners of a ring during layer-by-layer rotation",
    "rotating counterclockwise instead of clockwise by reversing before transposing rather than after"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a square grid of integers representing an image.\n\n**Rotate the image 90 degrees clockwise.**\n\nThe real interview constraint is to do this in place without allocating a second grid, but for this exercise just return the rotated grid.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "matrix is n x n (square)",
      "1 <= n <= 20",
      "-1000 <= matrix[i][j] <= 1000"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a 1x1 matrix (nothing changes)",
      "a 2x2 matrix (smallest case with an actual rotation)",
      "a matrix where every value is identical",
      "negative numbers"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "matrix = [[1,2,3],[4,5,6],[7,8,9]]",
        "output": "[[7,4,1],[8,5,2],[9,6,3]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[7,4,1],[8,5,2],[9,6,3]]",
        "explanation": "the first column, read bottom to top, becomes the first row after rotating clockwise"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "matrix = [[1,2],[3,4]]",
        "output": "[[3,1],[4,2]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[3,1],[4,2]]",
        "explanation": ""
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "matrix = [[5]]",
        "output": "[[5]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[5]]",
        "explanation": "a single cell is unchanged by rotation"
      }
